  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Monday...\n\nIsolated to scattered showers associated with a negatively tilted\ntrough are lifting north and east through south-central Montana \nearly this morning. While these showers will likely initially \ndecrease in coverage over the plains due to downslope winds off \nthe mountains behind a cold front passage, showers are expected to\nincrease in coverage once again later this morning over central \nand eastern Montana. By mid-day, the majority of the showers will \nhave lifted out of southern Montana into northern Montana, \nhowever, isolated showers over the mountains will continue as a \nsecondary low amplitude trough moves through the region. With the \nprecipitation today, amounts will generally be light, with a few \nlocations generally north and east of Billings potentially seeing \nup to a couple tenths of an inch of liquid. While rain will be the\npredominant precipitation type over the plains, snow levels \ndropping near 5,000 feet will allow for snow in the mountains. \nWith this, a couple inches of snow are forecast mainly on the west\nfacing slopes of the Absaroka-Beartooth and Crazy Mountains early\nthis morning.\n\nAlong with showers, this weather system is expected to bring \nwindy conditions to much of the area today. While the initial cold\nfront will mainly bring windy conditions to the mountains and \nmaybe some of the higher hills tonight, winds are expected to \nquickly increase across the plains this morning as the nocturnal \ninversion erodes. Look for peak wind gusts to be in the 20 to 40 \nmph range for most, with 40 to 50 mph over the foothills west of \nBillings. Cannot rule out an isolated higher wind gust around Big \nTimber and Harlowton, though the chance of seeing a 60 mph wind \ngust remains low. With this, the strongest wind gusts are expected\nthis morning for most, though far eastern Montana could see the \nstrongest winds during the early afternoon. Wind will then \ngradually decrease through the afternoon and evening today. \n\nThe forecast for Wednesday into Friday looks mainly dry with \nseasonable temperatures as a ridge of high pressure nudges into \nthe region and the winds aloft become southwesterly. While breezy \nconditions will exist at times, no strong winds are forecast. By \nlate Friday, the chance of precipitation returns to portions of \nsouth-central/western Montana as a shortwave trough lifts through \nthe region. \n\nLate this weekend into early next week, unsettled conditions may \nreturn as ensembles suggest a low pressure system could develop \nover the Rocky Mountains, just exactly where remains very \nuncertain. LA",
